@meta
id: 32-14
title: Conflict — neither side is the answer
chapter: 32
type: scenario
skills: conflict-resolution, range-pattern, global-delete, search-slash
par: 113
minutes: 13
vscode: three panes, and the merge editor cannot express this
open: src/domain/order/order.provider.ts src/configuration/registry.ts src/infrastructure/services/audit.service.ts src/infrastructure/services/metrics.service.ts
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
Third merge, and the hardest kind: one of the hunks has no correct
answer on either side.

    $ git merge main
    CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in src/domain/order/order.provider.ts
    CONFLICT (content): Merge conflict in src/configuration/registry.ts

Four hunks. Three of them are "both sides added something and both are
wanted". The fourth is not.

`main` renamed `cancel` to `cancelOrder` — that rename is already
everywhere else in the codebase, so the name has to survive. Your branch
replaced the repository update with `markCancelled`, which is the whole
point of your branch, so the body has to survive. The merged method is
`main`'s signature with your body, and neither block in the file says
that.

The two services are open for reference and do not change.

Sequence matters in this one. Resolve the hunk that needs thought first,
while you can still see which block is which, and sweep the "keep both"
hunks afterwards.

@keys
| `?^<<<` | search backwards to the start of this conflict |
| `:.,/^=/d` | delete from here down to the separator |
| `ciw` | change the word under the cursor |
| `:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d` | delete every marker line left in the file |

@hints
Four hunks, two tools. Work out which hunk is the odd one before you
delete anything, and do that one first: once you have run the marker
sweep you can no longer tell which lines came from which side.
--
For the odd hunk, keep the lower block — it is the one with the body you
want — and fix its signature afterwards. Deleting the upper block is one
range command: from the `<<<<<<<` line down to the `=======`.
--
`/markCancelled` `Enter` puts you inside the hunk that matters. `?^<<<`
`Enter` gets you to its top. `:.,/^=/d` removes `main`'s block, and the
cursor lands on the signature you now have to rename. `/^>>>` `Enter`
`dd` takes the last marker, and `:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d` clears what is left.

@solution
`/markCancelled` `Enter` `?^<<<` `Enter` `:.,/^=/d` `ww` `ciwcancelOrder`
`Esc` `/^>>>` `Enter` `dd` `:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d`, then `:bn`
`:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d` `:wqa`. 98 keystrokes.

**Navigate to the hunk by its content, not by its markers.** `/^<<<` from
the top of the file lands on the constructor hunk, which is not the one
you want to think about. `/markCancelled` lands you inside the hunk that
needs a decision, and `?^<<<` walks up to its top. Searching for the
thing you care about and then backing out to the structure around it is
worth having as a reflex — it is the same move as landing on a function
by name and then pressing `[{`.

**Keep the bigger side, patch the smaller difference.** Your body is five
lines; `main`'s contribution to the merged method is one word. Deleting
`main`'s block and retyping one identifier is far less work, and far less
error-prone, than keeping `main`'s block and reconstructing your body
inside it. Whenever both sides must contribute, ask which side is closer
to the answer and start from that one.

**Then sweep.** With the thinking hunk resolved, the remaining three are
all "both sides, in order, no spacing to preserve", which is exactly
`:g/^[<>=]\{7\}/d` — once per file. Doing the sweep first would have
merged both blocks of the method hunk into one file that defines `cancel`
and `cancelOrder` back to back, and you would have had to work out which
lines came from where with the markers already gone.

The general shape: in a multi-hunk merge, the hunks are not equally hard.
Find the one that needs a decision, make it while the markers are still
telling you who wrote what, and let a single command clean up the rest.

